Localised First-Degree Burn
Common Infections and Afflictions
Erythematous dry skin, Localized pain, Absence of blisters

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-021
Reason for Visit: Acute severe pain following a hot liquid spill on the forearm.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting an acute thermal injury from a boiling water spill, experiencing intense localized pain and distress. An urgent home assessment is clinically mandated to accurately assess the total body surface area (TBSA) involved, definitively rule out partial-thickness or full-thickness dermal involvement that would necessitate specialized burn unit referral, and immediately institute aggressive analgesia. Delaying this assessment poses an unacceptable risk of misclassifying a severe burn and prolonging acute suffering, strictly fulfilling the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: I spilled boiling water from the kettle on my arm, and the skin feels like it's continuously on fire.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 45 minutes prior following brief, accidental exposure to hot liquid (boiling water) on the left volar forearm. The patient immediately applied cool running tap water for 15 minutes. Currently reports moderate to severe localized pain described as a persistent burning sensation. The patient notes distinct erythema of the affected skin, warmth to touch over the burned area, and mild localized edema. There is a definitive dry appearance without blister formation, indicating an intact epidermal layer. Pain is exacerbated by ambient air currents across the skin. Denies inhalation injury, facial involvement, or concurrent trauma.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently favoring the uninjured arm due to significant pain with movement.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. Accidental mechanism reported; no safety hazards observed in the kitchen.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Visibly distressed, holding the left arm elevated and away from the body. Mild tachycardia consistent with acute pain.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. Normal capillary refill in the surrounding uninjured tissue would assist in confirming the diagnosis of superficial injury with intact perfusion, which is clinically evident.
Physical Exam: Left volar forearm demonstrates a 10cm x 8cm patch of uniform, bright erythema. The epidermal layer is entirely intact with a completely dry appearance and absolute absence of blisters or bullae. The affected skin demonstrates brisk and complete blanching upon light pressure. Mild, localized tissue edema is present. The burn affects approximately 1% Total Body Surface Area (TBSA). Capillary refill in the left digits is <2 seconds. Sensation is fully intact to light touch. Range of motion at the wrist and elbow is full but limited voluntarily by pain.
Environmental Observations: Kitchen is clean; the electric kettle cord is safely positioned away from the counter edge.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has not yet taken any oral analgesia.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Localised First-Degree Burn - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Second-Degree (Partial-Thickness) Burn - Ruled out definitively due to the complete absence of blister formation, the completely dry (rather than weeping/exudative) wound bed, and brisk blanching.
Cellulitis - Unlikely given the instantaneous onset linked precisely to a thermal trigger, rather than a gradual spreading erythema over days.
Chemical Burn - Ruled out by the explicit history of a thermal mechanism (boiling water) rather than exposure to caustic household or industrial agents.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The acute presentation of a thermal injury required immediate on-site clinical evaluation to accurately stage the depth and extent of the burn. Direct physical examination confirmed a superficial epidermal burn (first-degree) without blistering or compromise of deep dermal structures. By directly assessing the wound, initiating potent oral analgesia, and applying appropriate soothing topical dressings in the home, an unnecessary and highly distressing transfer to a hospital emergency department was successfully averted. This immediate clinical staging and pain management could not safely or humanely be delayed until the next standard operating period, satisfying MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered Ibuprofen 400mg and Paracetamol 1000mg orally stat for immediate analgesia. Recommended liberal application of a fragrance-free, aloe vera-based gel or bland emollient (e.g., Sorbolene) to the affected area three times daily to soothe the intact epidermis.
Positive Behavioral Change: To rapidly decrease the stinging sensation caused by air hitting the sensitive skin, apply a loose covering of plastic cling film directly over the burned area for the first 24 hours. The plastic film prevents ambient air currents from physically stimulating the exposed, hypersensitive nerve endings, functioning as a highly effective, non-stick pain barrier.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 3 days only if pain worsens or if delayed blistering occurs.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the skin begins to form large fluid-filled blisters, if the skin turns leathery, white, or black, or if you develop a fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis reflects the current burn depth; thermal injuries can occasionally declare their full depth over 24 to 48 hours.
Clinical Course: The acute pain will peak within the first 24 hours and gradually subside. The erythema will resolve, followed by mild peeling of the top layer of skin. Complete healing is expected within three to six days without scarring.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if the redness begins to spread aggressively up your arm, or if the burn starts to ooze thick, foul-smelling yellow pus.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The boiling water only damaged the very top protective layer of your skin. Because there are no blisters, the skin's barrier is still intact, meaning antibiotics and specialized burn creams are entirely unnecessary and may cause irritation.
Actionable Steps: Do not apply ice directly to the burn, as extreme cold causes secondary frostbite damage to the already compromised tissue. Continue to wash the area normally with lukewarm water and mild soap, gently patting it dry rather than aggressively rubbing.

